From edafa28bf48e4da7547c86b779af8fa8716aee9d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: halt95 <halt95@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 10:16:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 55/76] v2-ppfix2: fail closed on the V2 align path, and test
 the row as well as the table (Astra FIX-FIRST)

Astra's review (ASTRA-PPFIX2.md, 2026-09-15) returned FIX-FIRST: mechanism and
approach confirmed, two blockers. Both are addressed here.

1. FAIL CLOSED (Astra finding 1). `_populate_metadata` warned but still set
   `is_initialized = True`, and `run_fused_postprocess_align` then selected the
   gathered pointers with `BT_ROW_IS_REQ=False` -- i.e. the candidate
   deliberately kept a path to the exact rc1 corruption it exists to remove.
   The align launcher now raises RuntimeError instead: there is no safe
   fallback, so a context without the persistent tables is a caller bug, not a
   degraded mode. The raise happens before the accepted-token snapshot copy, so
   a failed launch mutates nothing. `BT_ROW_IS_REQ` is now unconditionally True
   on that path and `block_table_ptrs` is unconditionally the persistent set.

   `initialize_from_forward_context` keeps `persistent_block_tables` optional:
   the V1 callers (`preprocess_mamba` -> `run_fused_precopy`,
   `postprocess_mamba_all` -> `run_fused_postprocess`) never reach the align
   post-process and must keep working. Its warning now says what will happen
   rather than implying a silent degrade. `run_fused_precopy` is unaffected and
   has a test proving it still launches on an unregistered context.

   The five `initialize_from_forward_context` call sites in
   tests/v1/worker/test_mamba_utils.py now pass the same tensor as both the
   gathered and the persistent table. That is accurate for V1, where batch
   order == req-slot order, and keeps those contexts out of the raising branch
   if a test ever drives the align launcher.

2. THE REGRESSION TEST MISSED HALF THE FIX (Astra finding 2). The deferred
   mapping was [0, 1, 2] with the only copying request at batch row 0 / req
   slot 0, so `batch_idx == req_idx` and the row index was unobservable:
   selecting the persistent pointers while wrongly leaving `BT_ROW_IS_REQ=False`
   passed every test. Confirmed by mutation on this tree.

   `_build_scenario` now takes the mapping, and a new case uses batch row 0 ->
   request slot 5. The four possible coordinates resolve to four different
   block ids, so every wrong combination is caught:

       persistent[req_idx=5]    correct     60, 61
       persistent[batch_idx=0]  wrong row   10, 11
       gathered[batch_idx=0]    rc1         30, 31  (r2, left by the T-1 gather)
       gathered[req_idx=5]      wrong both  0, 0    (never written)

   The test also asserts the `BT_ROW_IS_REQ` constexpr itself, not only its
   effect. Mutation-checked on this tree: forcing `BT_ROW_IS_REQ=False` fails
   the new test alone; swapping back to the gathered pointers fails both row
   tests; the restored tree is green.

   Two more cases: the align launcher raises without persistent tables and
   enqueues nothing, and the pre-copy still launches on such a context.

3. GPU CORRECTNESS TEST (Astra (g).1/(g).2).
   tests/v1/worker/gpu/test_pp_deferred_mamba_gpu.py launches the real kernel
   on the same non-identity mapping and checks bytes, not arguments:
   positive-bias copy (num_accepted 4 -> token_bias 3, with the conv source
   column, the temporal source column `src_col + bias` and the destination
   column all distinct, so a wrong row cannot hide behind a coincidental
   self-copy); zero-bias whole-window migration; the `src == dst && bias == 0`
   exit, which must move nothing and still reset num_accepted to 1; a -1 (freed
   or reused) row, whose slot is given decisions that *would* copy so a missing
   guard shows up as a write; the same-step identity mapping; and the
   fail-closed raise on device. Every case asserts that every physical block
   outside the expected destination is bit-identical, which is the "victim
   untouched" check: request slots own disjoint physical blocks, so a stray
   write is attributable.

   Gated behind CUDA *and* `VLLM_E2_GPU_TESTS=1`. the reference host always reports CUDA
   available and spends most of its time running qualification boots and
   sanitizer ladders, so `torch.cuda.is_available()` alone is not a safe gate
   for a suite that anyone might run at any time.

Test runs (CPU, the reference host; the GPU file skips):
  tests/v1/worker/gpu ................................ 7 passed, 6 skipped
  tests/v1/ple_offload ............................... 137 passed, 1 skipped,
                                                       1 failed (flake, below)
  tests/v1/worker + gpu -k "pp or mamba" ............. 135 passed, 6 skipped,
                                                       3 failed (pre-existing)
  worker/gpu + mamba_utils + mamba_hybrid_model_state
    + pp_utils + gpu_block_table ..................... 68 passed, 6 skipped

The three "pp or mamba" failures are the same ones verified identical on the
v2-rc1 tree in the previous commit (two EADDRINUSE on the hard-coded port
12345, one `SimpleNamespace has no attribute 'ple_serving'`). The ple_offload
failure is
test_spawn_bootstrap_cuda.py::test_parent_death_during_config_reconstruction_exits_child,
a parent-death timing test that passed twice in isolation immediately after;
the box was running a compute-sanitizer ladder at the time. It passed in the
previous run of the same suite on this branch.

Astra's corrections to the previous commit message and report, recorded here:
"280 lines later" is wrong -- 1644 -> 1718 is 74 source lines, and :1442 is
inside the `prepare_attn` the latter calls; "no new device buffer" is wrong --
there is one new int64[num_groups] pointer tensor, allocated once; the ~12
copies per boot is an estimate from request shapes, not an observed count of
corrupting writes; and block lifetime under prefix-cache reassignment
(including the speculative `src_col + token_bias` source at
mamba_utils.py:353) is an open invariant that neither this fix nor the snapshot
ring establishes.
